gIH(){return"Sleep Science"},
gIF(){return"Evidence-based practices"},
gIG(){return"Research highlights from peer-reviewed studies. Tap any card to expand."},
gIE(){return"General information, not medical advice. If poor sleep is affecting your health or mood, talk to a doctor."},
gID(){return"These tips summarise current research for informational purposes. Persistent sleep difficulties should be discussed with a healthcare professional."},
gE2(){return"DOI / URL copied to clipboard"},
gIA(){return"Keep a consistent sleep schedule"},
gIz(){return"Going to bed and waking at the same time every day, including weekends, anchors your circadian rhythm. Research on circadian hygiene shows this is one of the strongest predictors of sleep quality. Even a single night of schedule shift can delay melatonin onset by hours."},
gIC(){return"Dim screens 60\u201390 min before bed"},
gIB(){return"Evening use of illuminated screens was associated with worse scores on every sleep parameter studied, including longer time to fall asleep, more night awakenings, and shorter total sleep. Blue-enriched light suppresses melatonin and shifts your circadian clock later. Use night mode, amber glasses, or, best of all, a book."},
gIo(){return"Cut caffeine 8\u201310 hours before bedtime"},
gIn(){return"Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, the chemical signal that builds sleep pressure through the day. Its half-life is roughly 5\u20136 hours, meaning a 3 PM coffee still has substantial activity at 11 PM. Evening caffeine also directly suppresses melatonin secretion and delays your circadian clock, compounding the effect beyond simple stimulation."},
gIq(){return"Cool your bedroom to ~18\xb0C / 65\xb0F"},
gIp(){return"Core body temperature must drop about 1\u20132\xb0C to initiate sleep. A cool environment (ideally 16\u201319\xb0C) accelerates this drop. Hot baths or showers 1\u20132 hours before bed are paradoxically helpful: the subsequent rapid heat loss from the skin actively pulls core temperature down and helps you fall asleep faster."},
gIu(){return"Exercise regularly, but not too late"},
gIt(){return"Regular physical activity is robustly associated with better sleep quality and duration. However, vigorous exercise within 2\u20133 hours of bedtime raises core temperature and cortisol, which can delay sleep onset. Morning and afternoon exercise is ideal; moderate evening walks are generally fine."},
gIs(){return"Sleep debt: recovery takes longer than you think"},
gIr(){return"A scientific review found that recovering from even one hour of sleep debt takes approximately four days of extended sleep. Chronic sleep restriction impairs cognition, mood, and metabolism in ways that feel \u201cnormal\u201d because adaptation masks the impairment, but performance tests reveal ongoing deficits. Weekend catch-up sleep partially helps, but doesn\u2019t undo all metabolic costs."},
gIw(){return"Eat lighter in the evening"},
gIv(){return"Having your largest meal at dinner is associated with shorter sleep and longer time to fall asleep. A 2024 meta-analysis found that higher adherence to a Mediterranean-style diet (whole grains, vegetables, healthy fats) was associated with fewer insomnia symptoms. Avoid heavy, high-glycemic foods in the 2\u20133 hours before bed."},
gIy(){return"Build a wind-down ritual"},
gIx(){return"A consistent pre-bed routine signals to your nervous system that sleep is coming. This can be as simple as 20 minutes of reading, gentle stretching, or a brief meditation. The key is repetition: the ritual itself becomes a conditioned cue that reduces arousal. Avoid emotionally activating content (news, arguments, work email) in the final hour."},
gGY(){return"Skip"},
gGX(){return"Next"},
gGZ(){return"Get started"},
gGQ(){return"Sleep debt, in plain terms"},
gGP(){return"Sleep debt is the gap between the sleep you need and the sleep you actually got. Most adults do best with 7 to 9 hours, and you set your own nightly target (8 hours to start). Lunaris adds up the difference so you can see whether you are running behind."},
gGS(){return"Log a night"},
gGR(){return"After you wake up, tap the + button on the Tonight or Log tab. Enter when you went to bed, when you woke, and optionally whether you woke up in the night and how well you slept. Tap a night to correct it. Change your nightly target from the Log tab (8 hours to start)."},
gGU(){return"Reading the numbers"},
gGT(){return"Both debt figures count only nights you logged, so a day without an entry never counts against you. One covers the last 14 days, the other everything you have logged. A minus means you are behind, a plus means you are ahead."},
gGW(){return"Your data stays here"},
gGV(){return"Everything is stored on this device only: no account and no network. The More tab can copy a backup you can paste back later, or a CSV for a spreadsheet. Lunaris is a personal tracker, not medical advice."},
